  ('72563287-f9d1-575e-8fe3-00e61a9928d3', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'systems-linear'), null, 'hard',
   $q$For what value of $k$ does the system $kx + 6y = 4$ and $2x + 3y = 5$ have no solution?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$k = 2$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"matched-x-coefficients-without-scaling"},{"text":"$k = -4$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-on-slope"},{"text":"$k = 1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"inverted-scale-factor"},{"text":"$k = 4$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$k = 4$$q$, $q$Multiply the second equation by $2$: $4x + 6y = 10$. If $k = 4$, the first equation is $4x + 6y = 4$, and the same expression cannot equal both $4$ and $10$, so the lines are parallel with no solution. The choice $k = 2$ copies the $x$ coefficient without scaling it by the same factor of $2$ that turns $3y$ into $6y$.$q$, 'draft', null)
