  ('855ad7a6-885b-5823-bacd-bfc74c40ea69', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'systems-linear'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The system $2x - 5y = 3$ and $ax + 10y = b$ has infinitely many solutions. What are the values of $a$ and $b$?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$a = 4$ and $b = -6$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"scaled-only-some-terms"},{"text":"$a = -4$ and $b = -6$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$a = 4$ and $b = 6$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dropped-negative-scale-factor"},{"text":"$a = -4$ and $b = 3$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"did-not-scale-constant"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$a = -4$ and $b = -6$$q$, $q$For infinitely many solutions the second equation must be a multiple of the first. Since $-5y$ becomes $10y$, the factor is $-2$: $-2(2x - 5y) = -2(3)$ gives $-4x + 10y = -6$. So $a = -4$ and $b = -6$. The pair $a = 4$ and $b = 6$ multiplies by $2$, which would give $-10y$, not $10y$.$q$, 'draft', null)
